# IMPERIA CW — Cold War: West Germany integration from quaZa e45483ff.
"""Flavor texts: the chancellery pillar, part 1."""
D = {
"chancellery": "Under Horst Ehmke the Palais Schaumburg is meant to become the nerve centre of a modern state: planning staffs, early computers, a chief of staff who knows every file before the minister does. The idea is that the Federal Republic can be governed rather than merely administered. The ministers, the coalition partner and the Bundestag all have their own views on that.",
"situation_centre": "Until now the chancellor learned of a crisis from the morning papers or from a telephone call at midnight. A situation room in the chancellery changes that: BND assessments, the cables from the Permanent Representation in East Berlin and the crisis staff's lines all come together in one windowless room beneath the Palais. Whoever controls the information controls the government's first hour.",
"mehr_demokratie_wagen": "'We want to dare more democracy.' Willy Brandt's first government statement promises a society that is more open, more just and more willing to take risks with freedom - after twenty years of Union chancellors, a change of era. The social-liberal coalition holds the Bundestag by a handful of seats, and every reform it promises will have to survive a Bundesrat, a Constitutional Court and an opposition convinced that the state belongs to it.",
"voting_age_18": "Students who marched against the emergency laws, apprentices who joined the unions at sixteen, conscripts who can be sent to the border but not to the ballot box: lowering the voting age to eighteen brings the generation of 1968 into the electorate. The SPD expects their gratitude. The young voters have not promised it.",
"bafoeg": "Barely one student in twenty is the child of a worker. The Federal Training Assistance Act changes the arithmetic: grants for anyone whose parents cannot pay, a university system opened to the whole nation and a pool of engineers, teachers and doctors that the economy badly needs. The universities will be overcrowded within a decade - which is, in a sense, the point.",
"works_constitution": "The Works Constitution Act of 1972 gives the works councils a say in hiring, overtime and the layout of the shop floor, and lets the unions into the factory gates. For the DGB it is a down payment on economic democracy; for the employers' associations, a step towards the trade union state. On the shop floor it means one thing above all: the foreman no longer decides alone.",
"family_law_reform": "The Civil Code still assumes that a wife keeps house and needs her husband's permission to take a job. The reform rewrites marriage law from the ground up: divorce on the breakdown of the marriage rather than on proven guilt, maintenance after divorce, and no single model of family life written into law. The churches protest from the pulpit. Many families had stopped living by the old rules long ago.",
"pension_reform": "The dynamic pension of 1957 tied old age to the growth of wages; the reform of 1972 goes further, with a flexible retirement age from sixty-three and a minimum pension for those who earned too little to live on. In the Bundestag the parties outbid each other with generosity in an election year. The bill will be presented later, to another generation.",
"codetermination": "Workers and shareholders sit opposite each other on the supervisory boards of every company with more than two thousand employees - almost as equals. The chairman, appointed by the shareholders, keeps the casting vote, because the FDP insisted on it and the coalition needed the FDP. The employers take the law to Karlsruhe; the unions call it a half-measure. Both accept it in the end.",
"modell_deutschland": "While Britain strikes, Italy is shaken by bombs and America struggles with inflation, Helmut Schmidt has a slogan for his country: Modell Deutschland. A hard currency, social partners who settle in the end, an export industry that sells to the whole world. It is a model of crisis management, not of vision - and the chancellor would not have it any other way.",
"kanalarbeiter": "They call themselves the Kanalarbeiter, the sewer workers, because they do the party's dirty work below ground: right-wing Social Democrats from the trade unions and the Seeheim Circle who keep the SPD on the side of NATO, nuclear power and the chancellor. Backing them means a quieter party conference - and a party that slowly stops listening to its own youth.",
"juso_left": "The Young Socialists want to overcome capitalism by structural reform, nationalise the key industries and leave the arms race to others. Giving them a voice in the party means energy, new members and a bridge to the movements in the streets. It also means a coalition partner in the FDP who reads the Jusos' resolutions very carefully.",
"freiburg_theses": "In Freiburg the Free Democrats write themselves a new soul: social liberalism. Property that carries obligations, co-determination, environmental protection long before anyone else speaks of it, and a liberal state that guarantees freedom in society rather than merely from the state. For a few years the FDP is the most interesting party in Bonn.",
"kiel_theses": "The market has returned to the Free Democrats. In Kiel the party rediscovers lower taxes, less regulation and its business wing - and with it a partner on the other side of the chamber. The social-liberal coalition was a marriage of reason, say the economic liberals. Reason now suggests a divorce.",
"genscher_foreign_office": "Hans-Dietrich Genscher will serve longer as foreign minister than anyone in the Western world: détente without illusions, Western integration without servility, an endless round of visits from which he returns before anyone notices he has left. In Washington they invent a word for his policy - Genscherism - and they do not mean it kindly.",
"baum_liberal_interior": "Gerhart Baum is the first interior minister to see the security services as a danger to be controlled as much as an instrument to be used. Data protection, limits on the BKA's computers, the end of routine vetting for applicants to the civil service: the liberal answer to the German Autumn. The police unions are not convinced.",
"union_opposition": "For twenty years the CDU/CSU provided every chancellor. Now it sits on the opposition benches after losing power by a handful of seats, convinced that the state belongs to it and that the Eastern treaties are a sell-out of the German question. The Union has to decide what kind of opposition it wants to be - and whether it can bring down the government before the next election.",
"so_nicht": "'Not like this!' Rainer Barzel's line on the Eastern treaties is a masterpiece of tactical ambiguity: no blanket rejection of reconciliation with the East, but no ratification on these terms. It keeps the CDU and the CSU together, keeps the expellees' associations on board - and leaves open what the Union would actually do in government.",
"joint_resolution": "A way out for everyone: the Bundestag ratifies the treaties with Moscow and Warsaw, and all parties together adopt a resolution that the treaties do not anticipate a peace settlement and leave the German question open. The Union can abstain rather than vote no, the government gets its treaties, and the Constitutional Court gets a text to quote for decades.",
"party_reform_cdu": "Kurt Biedenkopf and Heiner Geißler turn the CDU from a chancellor's electoral association into a modern members' party: full-time staff in every district, a party academy, campaign planning by computer. Within a decade the membership has doubled. The old notables grumble about the young men from the Konrad-Adenauer-Haus. They also win elections.",
"change_1982": "The constructive vote of no confidence was designed in 1949 to prevent Weimar's chaos: a chancellor can only be removed if the Bundestag elects a successor in the same vote. When the FDP's economic liberals have had enough of the SPD's debts and its quarrels over missiles, that article becomes a path to power without an election - and a betrayal, say those who voted for the coalition.",
"spiritual_moral_turn": "Helmut Kohl promises more than a change of government: a geistig-moralische Wende, a spiritual and moral turn. Less state, more family, more enterprise, an unashamed loyalty to the West and an end to the cultural dominance of the generation of 1968. What exactly that means in practice remains, as so often with this chancellor, pleasantly unclear.",
"kohl_machine": "Kohl does not govern through his cabinet but through his party: a network of district chairmen whose birthdays he remembers, telephone calls late at night, favours given and remembered, and an almost superhuman patience with rivals who underestimate him. 'The Kohl system' is mocked by the intellectuals. It survives every one of them.",
"consolidation_course": "Count Lambsdorff's paper on the economy ended the social-liberal coalition; now it becomes government policy. Cuts to subsidies and social spending, lower taxes on companies, a more flexible labour market and a state that withdraws to its core tasks. The deficit falls, unemployment does not - and the unions prepare for a long fight.",
"pflegeversicherung": "Norbert Blüm wants a fifth pillar for the social insurance system: insurance for long-term care, so that old age does not end in poverty and the social welfare office. The employers will only agree if they are compensated, and so the Federal Republic abolishes a public holiday - the Day of Repentance and Prayer - to pay for it.",
"bitburg": "Forty years after the end of the war, the chancellor invites the American president to a military cemetery in Bitburg as a gesture of reconciliation. Then it emerges that members of the Waffen-SS are buried there. Washington is in uproar, veterans protest, and the chancellor refuses to change the programme. At home, a surprising number of Germans are on his side.",
"csu_bavaria": "Franz Josef Strauß runs the CSU as a state party with federal ambitions, a Bavarian kingdom with its own foreign policy and its own ideas about who should be chancellor. Keeping him inside the Union tent costs concessions on every major question. Letting him outside would cost a great deal more.",
"greens_parliament": "They arrive in the Bundestag in jumpers, with sunflowers and with their children: ecological, social, grassroots-democratic and non-violent. The Greens refuse to behave like a party, rotate their members of parliament every two years and bring the peace movement, the anti-nuclear protests and the women's movement into the chamber. The old parties do not yet know whether to laugh or to worry.",
"realos": "Joschka Fischer and the pragmatists want to change the country from inside the government rather than from the street: coalitions with the SPD, budgets, compromises and eventually even NATO. The fundamentalists call it treason to the movement and leave in anger. The Greens that remain are smaller, more disciplined - and electable.",
"fundis": "No coalitions with the parties of the arms race, rotation of every mandate, withdrawal from NATO and an end to nuclear power without compromise. The fundamentalists keep the Greens pure and keep them in opposition. For their voters that is a programme; for the SPD, which needs a partner, it is a problem.",
"red_green_hesse": "December 1985: Joschka Fischer is sworn in as Hesse's environment minister in white trainers. The first red-green coalition in a German state is a fragile experiment - the Greens want the Hanau nuclear plants closed, the SPD wants to govern - but the photograph goes around the world. A movement has entered the state.",
"red_green_federal": "A generation that once marched against the state now governs it. SPD and Greens form the first red-green federal government: an exit from nuclear power, a new citizenship law, an ecological tax reform. Former street fighters become ministers, former pacifists will have to decide on war. Power, it turns out, changes those who hold it.",
"lebenspartnerschaft": "For almost a century German citizenship has been a matter of blood: the grandchild of a Volga German is a German, the son of a Turkish worker born in Duisburg is not. Red-green wants to change that and to give same-sex couples the right to a registered partnership. The Union collects signatures against dual citizenship - and wins a state election with them.",
"bundesrat_bargaining": "When the other camp holds the majority of the Länder, every major law ends up in the Mediation Committee, where state premiers and members of the Bundestag haggle behind closed doors until two in the morning. Nothing becomes law without the opposition's consent. The government learns that federalism is also a form of opposition.",
"federal_president": "Heinemann, Scheel, Carstens, Weizsäcker: the Federal President has little power and great authority. His election by the Federal Convention is a test of the coalitions to come, and his speeches can say what no chancellor dares. A president above the parties is a resource - if the government chooses him wisely.",
"weizsaecker_speech": "8 May 1985, forty years after the end of the war. Richard von Weizsäcker tells the Bundestag that it was a day of liberation - also for the Germans - and that whoever closes his eyes to the past becomes blind to the present. The speech is printed in millions of copies and taught in schools. The Republic has found a language for its history.",
"berlin_capital": "June 1991, the Bundestag in the old waterworks in Bonn. After eleven hours of debate, 338 to 320 members vote to move parliament and government to Berlin. For the Rhineland it is a shock; for the East, a promise. The move will take eight years, cost billions and change the character of the Republic - from the Bonn Republic to the Berlin Republic.",
"asylum_compromise": "Four hundred thousand asylum seekers in one year, hostels burning in Rostock and Hoyerswerda, a state that seems overwhelmed. Government and opposition agree to rewrite Article 16 of the Basic Law: the right to asylum remains, but whoever arrives through a safe third country or comes from a safe country of origin can be turned away. A compromise that some call necessary and others the end of a promise.",
"vergangenheitsbewaeltigung": "The Majdanek trial in Düsseldorf runs for five years; the American series 'Holocaust' is watched by twenty million West Germans in 1979 and does what decades of documentation could not; the Bundestag abolishes the statute of limitations for murder so that the perpetrators can still be tried. Coming to terms with the past is not a single act but a task without end.",
"barzel_cabinet": "Two votes - perhaps bought, nobody will ever know - have made Rainer Barzel chancellor. He must govern with a handful of defectors from the SPD and FDP, a Bundestag that is evenly split and a country that is not sure whether it has seen a change of government or a coup by procedure. The first months will decide whether Chancellor Barzel is an episode or an era.",
"barzel_west_first": "Barzel's Ostpolitik is the same as Brandt's in many respects - but in the reverse order: Washington's blessing first, then Moscow; a written reservation on German unity first, then signatures. The East calls it revanchism with better manners; the Americans are pleased to be asked; the expellees think the chancellor has not gone far enough.",
"barzel_social_market": "The new government stops the expensive reform projects of the social-liberal years and promises to keep the pension promises - no more, no less. The social market economy, says the chancellor, needs consolidation rather than experiments. The unions sense that the times are changing; the employers hope they are right.",
"csu_nationwide": "Wildbad Kreuth, November 1976: the CSU's members of parliament vote to end the joint parliamentary group with the CDU and to stand throughout the Federal Republic. This time the decision holds. The CDU founds a Bavarian branch in revenge. Two Union parties fight for the same voters - or, if they are clever, for different ones.",
"cdu_centre_course": "Without the CSU's right flank the CDU can move where its modernisers always wanted it to be: the political centre. It courts the FDP, the moderate SPD voter, the new middle classes in the suburbs. The conservatives who feel abandoned now have a party to go to - in Munich.",
"schmidt_minority": "The FDP ministers have resigned, the chancellor remains. Helmut Schmidt governs with a minority government - a novelty in Bonn, and a countdown. Every law needs a majority found anew, every budget is a gamble, and the opposition is preparing the vote that will bring it to power.",
"schmidt_snap_election": "The chancellor could wait for the opposition to bring him down. Or he could ask the Bundestag for a vote of confidence, lose it on purpose and let the Federal President dissolve parliament. It is constitutionally dubious and politically daring - and it lets the voters, not the coalition committees, decide who governs.",
"strauss_model": "Chancellor Franz Josef Strauß: aerospace and nuclear power, low taxes and a strong police, a loud voice in Washington and an even louder one in his own cabinet. What Bavaria has become under the CSU - a farming state turned into a high-tech region - is now to become the model for the Federal Republic.",
"strauss_aerospace": "Strauß chairs the supervisory board of Airbus and wants a German aerospace champion to rival Boeing and Dassault: Messerschmitt-Bölkow-Blohm in Ottobrunn, fighter jets, satellites, helicopters. Defence contracts and research money flow south. In Hamburg and Bremen, where the other plants are, people ask why everything always ends up in Bavaria.",
"strauss_east_credit": "Of all people, the man who denounced Ostpolitik as a sell-out arranges a loan of a billion marks for Erich Honecker - guaranteed by Bonn, negotiated with Alexander Schalck-Golodkowski. In return the GDR dismantles the automatic firing devices on the border. Only Nixon could go to China, they say in Bonn. Only Strauß could go to East Berlin.",
"spaeth_high_tech": "Lothar Späth, 'the cleverle' from Stuttgart, has turned Baden-Württemberg into the most innovative state in the Federal Republic: research parks, technology transfer, venture capital, Daimler and Bosch working with the universities. Now the model is to go federal - and the Federal Republic is to become the Silicon Valley of Europe.",
"spaeth_slow_unity": "Chancellor Späth will not promise blooming landscapes. Unity, he says, has to be earned and paid for: step by step, mark by mark, with a currency conversion that the East's factories can survive and a timetable that the West's taxpayers can accept. It is the voice of reason. Whether reason wins elections in a year of euphoria is another question.",
"lafontaine_keynes": "Chancellor Oskar Lafontaine believes in demand: lower interest rates, more public investment, higher wages for those who spend them. He also believes the Bundesbank is too independent and says so in public. In London the tabloids call him the most dangerous man in Europe; in Frankfurt, the central bankers prepare for a long war.",
"lafontaine_cautious_unity": "In 1990 Lafontaine warned that unity would be paid for with tax rises and unemployment, and lost the election for it. Now he is chancellor and must prove that it could have been done more cheaply: slower, with more help for the East's industry and fewer promises. His party wants him to be right. His country wants him to be wrong.",
"grand_coalition": "When neither camp has a majority, the two big parties govern together - as in 1966. A grand coalition can change the Basic Law and pass reforms that neither side could manage alone. It also leaves the opposition to the small parties and the street, and both have a habit of growing.",
"national_right": "There are votes to the right of the Union - and a government that needs them. A chancellor who speaks of 'foreign infiltration', 'red terror' and 'national dignity' finds an audience that Bonn's political class has kept out of the mainstream since 1949. Each speech moves the line of what may be said a little further. Nobody in the cabinet seems to notice where it is heading.",
"order_above_all": "Terrorists, demonstrators, 'parasites on the welfare state': the government promises order above everything, and the police get whatever they ask for. Raids on bookshops, bans on demonstrations, emergency powers tested in practice. The Basic Law's critics are no longer only on the left - and the Republic's defenders begin to look nervously at Karlsruhe.",
"street_and_ballot": "Torchlight marches in provincial towns, a 'national' newspaper at every kiosk, veterans' associations in the front row of the rallies. The far right no longer hides in back rooms; it marches with the government's blessing. And the other side takes to the streets as well: trade unions, churches, students. The Republic is being contested in the market squares.",
"crisis_of_the_republic": "Bonn is not Weimar, the textbooks said. Now the government argues that the Basic Law has made the state defenceless against its enemies, and that only a state of emergency can save it. The Constitutional Court in Karlsruhe is watching; so are the generals, the trade unions and the Allied embassies. The question is no longer whether the Republic is in crisis, but who will define the crisis.",
"emergency_decree": "The emergency laws of 1968 were written for war - for the moment when the Bundestag can no longer meet. The government declares that the war has come from within. Decrees replace laws, the small Joint Committee replaces the Bundestag, and the Basic Law's safeguards are suspended one article at a time. It is all perfectly legal, the ministry insists.",
"national_directorate": "The Bundestag is adjourned 'until order is restored'. A National Directorate assumes the powers of the chancellor and the federal president and promises a national renewal. The black, red and gold flag still flies over the Palais Schaumburg - but over a different state. Somewhere, the Allied High Commissioners' old files are being taken out of the archives.",
"purge_institutions": "Judges, editors, professors, officers, teachers: the lists are drawn up in the ministries. Whoever signs the declaration of loyalty to the national renewal keeps his post. The others leave - for prison, for exile or for the West's embassies. The institutions the Republic built over thirty years are emptied in a few months.",
"national_rearmament": "No more NATO quotas, no more integrated command, no more ceiling on the army agreed in Paris in 1954: the Directorate wants an army of its own size, under its own command and for its own purpose. In Brussels, in Washington and in Moscow the same question is asked in different languages: what purpose?",
"renounce_treaties": "Moscow, Warsaw, Prague, the Basic Treaty: 'treaties of renunciation', the Directorate calls them, signed by a republic that no longer exists and binding on no one. The notes are delivered to the embassies in a single morning. Twenty years of détente are torn up - and with them every guarantee that Germany's neighbours had learned to rely on.",
"old_borders": "The Directorate claims the 'Soviet Zone' as German territory under foreign occupation and lays down a war goal against East Berlin. It is not only a challenge to the GDR but to every army of the Warsaw Pact - and to every NATO government that never signed up to fight for this. Europe holds its breath.",
"restore_basic_law": "The Directorate has failed - or has understood in time that it will. The Bundestag is recalled to Bonn, the decrees are lifted, the prisoners are released and free elections are announced. The Republic returns, battered and humbled. Whether the world believes it, and whether the Germans believe it themselves, is a different question.",
}
